+### The predictions gate (`hive-launch` refuses what `hive-score` cannot read)
+
+`hive-launch` and `hive-score` share one parser, so the gate and the scorer cannot disagree.
+The launcher **refuses**, before any spine write, when an order has a `## Predictions` section
+in which **none** of the three scored fields parses — printing the four literal bullet labels
+and the offending lines. Fix the labels in the order (commit + push), then re-run; `--anyway`
+launches it as-is, and says out loud that it will score `unpredicted`.
+
+Three cases deliberately do **not** refuse:
+
+- an order with **no `## Predictions` section** — that gap is already a recorded metric, and an
+  order launched unpredicted on purpose must never need an override to get out;
+- an order carrying the protocol's **declared-unpredicted one-liner** (a line containing
+  `deliberately unpredicted`, and why) — the launch announces that it read the declaration;
+- a **partial** section (1–2 of 3) — it launches with a loud warning naming the fields that did
+  not parse.
+
+**Three fields are scored, not four.** The authoring protocol requires four bullets;
+`- Named risks:` is written by every author and read by no instrument. It is documented as
+unscored rather than quietly wired in — what a launch gate scores is the operator's call, not the
+tooling's. The three scored labels are literal:
+
+```
+- Expected effort: <range> — <this project's median × a justified multiplier>
+- Expected questions: <count or range> — <named seams, or a designed round-trip>
+- Expected review outcome: <clean | minor rework | rework> — <why>
+```
+
+`calibration.md` distinguishes the ways an order can be unpredicted, because they are different
+facts: `unpredicted (no ## Predictions section)` is a gap, `unpredicted (section present, 0 of 3
+fields parsed)` is a prediction the instrument **lost** (a defect in the order's labels), and
+`unpredicted (declared: …)` is a deliberate choice. Conflating the first two is how an entire
+tenant project's first wave was lost silently (retro 2026-07-29 D1).
+
+**If an instrument's push is refused.** `hive-score`/`hive-metrics` commit locally and then push.
+On a push failure they **refuse rather than rebase** — they now run unattended on every close, and
+an automatic `pull --rebase` of a shared metrics path is an unsupervised history rewrite. Nothing
+is lost; the commit is in your clone and only the hub is behind. Look, then push by hand:
+
+```bash
+git -C ~/workspaces/hive log --oneline -- projects/<project>/metrics   # what you are about to send
+git -C ~/workspaces/hive fetch origin && git -C ~/workspaces/hive status -sb
+git -C ~/workspaces/hive pull --rebase && git -C ~/workspaces/hive push
+```
 
 Run identity is a committed per-project fact (`projects/<name>/project.conf`: `RUN_ID`,
 `CODE_REPO`); the deployment layer defaults to the operator-host layout and is env-overridable
