+# instrument-teeth — result
+
+**Order:** `projects/omegahive/orders/2026-07-29-instrument-teeth.md` ·
+**Deliverable:** one `scripts/` PR (branch `instrument-teeth`) + RUNBOOK touches.
+
+## What shipped
+
+Six changes across four scripts and two drills, plus the RUNBOOK. Every one of them turns a
+prose rule into either code or a checked moment, which is the retro's own test for whether a
+repair will survive a wave (D6).
+
+**1. One parser, two callers** — `scripts/hive-common.sh`. The prediction regexes now live in
+exactly one place: `parse_predictions <order-file>`, which sets `PRED_SECTION`, `PRED_BLOCK`,
+`PRED_DECLARED`, the three verbatim field slices, `PRED_FIELDS` and `PRED_MISSING`.
+`hive-score` and `hive-launch` both call it and neither carries a regex of its own. The four
+literal labels are pinned beside it as `PREDICTION_LABELS`, and the file says out loud that
+three of the four are scored.
+
+**2. `hive-launch` refuses an unparseable predictions section** — `scripts/hive-launch`. When an
+order has a `## Predictions` heading and **no** scored field parses, the launch refuses and
+prints the four literal labels, a note that `- Named risks:` is not scored, and the offending
+lines. `--anyway` (the existing throttle-override spelling) launches it and says what it will
+cost. The check sits immediately after the id guards — **before the WIP throttle, before the
+clones, before any emit** — so a refusal leaves the spine, the disk and the tmux server exactly
+as it found them. It reads the order and writes nothing back into it.
+
+Three cases launch cleanly, on purpose: no `## Predictions` section at all (silently — that gap
+is already a recorded metric); the protocol's declared-unpredicted one-liner (announced); a
+partial 1–2-of-3 section (a loud warning naming the fields that did not parse).
+
+**3. `hive-score` distinguishes absent from unparsed** — `scripts/hive-score`. Coverage is now
+five strings instead of three:
+
+| situation | recorded as |
+|---|---|
+| all three fields parse | `full` |
+| 1–2 parse | `partial (N of 3 fields predicted; not parsed: <names>)` |
+| no `## Predictions` section | `unpredicted (no \`## Predictions\` section)` |
+| section present, nothing parses | `unpredicted (section present, 0 of 3 fields parsed)` |
+| section declares the task unpredicted | `unpredicted (declared: …)` |
+
+The middle pair is the repair D1 asked for. The last row is a judgment call the order did not
+name explicitly and is flagged under Reflection.
+
+**4. `hive-score --review` validates** — accepts `clean | minor rework | rework`
+case-insensitively, stores lowercase, refuses anything else with a message naming the three.
+Validated at argument-parse time, before the spine read and before any append, so a typo costs a
+re-run and never a half-written entry.
+
+**5. `hive-close` regenerates `tasks.md`/`tasks.csv`** — alongside the score it already ran,
+retiring R6's surviving manual half. The ordering discipline `cli-qol` established is unchanged
+and asserted: the close emits first, and both instrument steps afterwards are loud and
+non-fatal. The metrics step runs even when scoring failed — they are independent artifacts.
+`--no-score` now skips both, and its message says so.
+
+**6. `commit_metrics` refuses rather than rebases** — `scripts/hive-common.sh`. The inherited
+`push → pull --rebase → push` retry is gone. On a push failure it commits locally, prints the
+inspect-then-push sequence, and exits non-zero. The Watch entry's trigger ("the first time it
+bites") is pre-empted rather than waited for, because change 5 raises its firing rate.
+
+**7. Drill-hygiene sweep** — reported in full below.
+
+**Documentation** — `projects/omegahive/RUNBOOK.md`: the launch bullet names the refusal and
+`--anyway`; a new *predictions gate* subsection covers what refuses, the three cases that do not,
+the literal labels, the coverage vocabulary, and the fact that `Named risks` is required and
+unscored; the close bullet names the metrics refresh and the `--review` vocabulary; the wave-close
+section drops `hive-metrics` from the ritual (it is now a repair, not a step); and the push
+refusal has its resolution steps.
+
+### The drill-hygiene sweep (order scope 7 — bounded to two named classes)
+
+**Files read as code:** `scripts/hive-metrics-drill.sh` in full (the order's named target), with
+`scripts/hive-tooling-drill.sh` used as the reference for both class definitions, since it
+already carries them as comments.
+
+**Class A — EXIT trap installed before the isolation it depends on: none found.** The class is
+real and expensive (`launch-pane-fix` shipped one where `cleanup`'s `tmux kill-server` would
+have reached the operator's live server), but it has no target here. `hive-metrics-drill.sh`'s
+`cleanup` has exactly one side effect, `rm -rf "$SANDBOX"`; `SANDBOX` is assigned at line 18 by
+`mktemp -d` and the trap is installed at line 54, so the value and the directory both exist
+before the trap can fire, and a failed `mktemp` aborts under `set -e` while no trap is
+installed. There is no `kill-server`, no container command, and no push to a shared remote in
+its cleanup — nothing whose blast radius depends on an isolation step. Stated rather than left
+silent, because "we looked and there is nothing" is the finding.
+
+**Class B — `cmd | grep -q` under `set -o pipefail`: three instances found, all three fixed.**
+The drill's three stop-line assertions:
+
+```bash
+check "hive-metrics contains no emit" "! code '$M' | grep -Eq '…emit…'"
+check "hive-score contains no emit"   "! code '$S' | grep -Eq '…emit…'"
+check "neither tool commits to git"   "! { code '$M'; code '$S'; } | grep -Eq 'git .*(commit|push)'"
+```
+
+`grep -q` exits at the first match and SIGPIPEs its upstream, which fails the pipeline under
+`pipefail`. What makes these worse than the `cli-qol` instance is the negation: `cli-qol`'s bug
+produced a false **FAIL** against a correct repository — loud, and it got found. These produce a
+false **PASS**, and they are the three assertions that certify the read-only stop-line the order
+itself names. "hive-score issues no events" going green *because the check broke* is the worst
+failure this file can have.
+
+They are **latent today, not live**: the upstream is a whole-file `grep -v` whose output
+(~15 KB and ~9 KB) still fits in a 64 KB pipe buffer, so every write completes before `grep -q`
+can close the read end and no SIGPIPE is delivered. That is a size, not an invariant —
+`hive-metrics` is 28 KB and growing. Fixed by reading the stripped source into variables once
+and matching with here-strings, so no pipeline exists to break.
+
+Two further Class-B *near*-instances were considered and deliberately left: `printf '%s' "$VAR"
+| grep -q` (roughly twenty sites) and `entry <task> | grep -q`. Their upstreams are a shell
+builtin and a small `awk`, both bounded by construction and far below the buffer, and — the
+reason it is safe to leave them — nearly all are positive assertions, where a spurious pipeline
+failure is a loud false FAIL rather than a silent false PASS. Churning twenty lines to no effect
+is not what the order asked for. The **new** code added by this task uses here-strings
+throughout, so the count does not grow.
+
+**Two third-class findings, reported and NOT fixed** (the order's stop-line: a third class is a
+finding for the report, not a scope expansion):
+
+1. **An aborted drill reports as green.** `cleanup` prints `PASS=<n> FAIL=0` on *any* exit,
+   including a `set -e` abort mid-run, and prints `FAILURES PRESENT` only when `FAIL > 0`.
+   Verified: injecting a failure at the first `hive-metrics` call gives exit status 1 and the
+   summary line `metrics drill: PASS=0 FAIL=0`. The exit status does distinguish the two, so a
+   CI caller is safe; the human-facing line — the one an operator reads — does not. This bit me
+   during this task: a broken `git clone` in a new section aborted the drill and printed
+   `PASS=127 FAIL=0`, which I nearly accepted as a pass. A one-line "reached the end" sentinel
+   would close it. Both drills share the shape.
+2. **`HIVE_RUN_ID` from the operator's environment defeats the fixture.** The drills override
+   `OPS_WS`, `OMEGA_DIR` and `HIVE_SPINE_JSON` but not `HIVE_RUN_ID`, which wins over
+   `project.conf` by the documented precedence rule. Verified:
+   `HIVE_RUN_ID=stray scripts/hive-metrics-drill.sh` gives `PASS=130 FAIL=7`. Loud rather than
+   silent, so it is a hermeticity gap rather than a defect — but a drill's isolation should not
+   depend on the operator's shell being clean.
+
+## How verified
+
+**`scripts/hive-metrics-drill.sh` — green, 137 PASS / 0 FAIL, exit 0.** Up from 101 at this
+task's baseline; 36 new assertions. This drill is hermetic by construction: a frozen JSON spine
+fixture via `HIVE_SPINE_JSON`, `OMEGA_DIR` pointed at an empty directory, no events issued and
+no run touched.
+
+New coverage, mapped to the order's definition of done:
+
+- **(c) one parser** — proved twice. *Structurally:* `Expected effort:` appears in exactly one
+  of the four scripts, `hive-launch` carries no field regex, and both callers invoke
+  `parse_predictions`. *Behaviourally:* six cases where the gate's verdict (read out of the
+  shared function) and the scorer's verdict (read out of what it actually wrote to
+  `calibration.md`) are asserted against the same input file.
+- **(d) coverage strings** — two new fixture tasks. `eta` carries a full section in the exact
+  `- **Effort:**` dialect from D1; `theta` carries the declared-unpredicted one-liner; `gamma`
+  (existing) has no section. All three are asserted to produce *different* strings, and `eta`'s
+  order is separately asserted to really carry a `## Predictions` heading — otherwise the test
+  proves only that two files have no predictions in them.
+- **(e) `--review`** — `Rework` → `rework`, `MINOR REWORK` → `minor rework`, `clean` → `clean`;
+  `bogus`, the prefix near-miss `rewor`, and bare `minor` all refused naming the three values;
